      "LoginRequest": {
        "properties": {
          "email": {
            "type": "string",
            "maxLength": 254,
            "minLength": 5,
            "title": "Email"
          },
          "password": {
            "type": "string",
            "maxLength": 72,
            "title": "Password"
          }
        },
        "type": "object",
        "required": [
          "email",
          "password"
        ],
        "title": "LoginRequest",
        "description": "Credentials posted to the sign in endpoint.\n\nThe address is checked structurally rather than with a full RFC 5322\nparser. Deliverability is proved by the verification email, not by a\nregular expression, so a validation library here would be a dependency\nthat buys nothing.\n\nThe password carries no minimum length. It deliberately did once, and that\nwas a defect: a wrong password of eleven characters came back as a 422\nnaming the length rule, while a wrong password of twelve came back as a 401.\nThat difference is an oracle. It tells an attacker something about the value\nthey submitted, and it contradicts control C-14 in the security section,\nwhich states that an unknown address and a wrong password are answered\nidentically in body, status and timing. Length policy applies when a\npassword is chosen, not when one is presented, so it lives in\n`security.hash_password` (BR-45) and not here. The maximum stays, because it\nis a property of bcrypt rather than a rule about the account, and it applies\nto the unknown address and the wrong password alike."
      },
